    <!-- Before Planting -->
    <string name="legal_q1_title">Is It Legal to Grow Sandalwood?</string>
    <string name="legal_q1_content">Yes. Growing sandalwood on private land is fully legal in Karnataka since the Karnataka Forest Amendment Act 2001. You do not need any permission just to plant trees. However harvesting and selling require separate permits.</string>
    <string name="legal_q2_title">Land Requirements</string>
    <string name="legal_q2_content">Your land must be privately owned patta land with a clear title. You must have a valid land ownership certificate issued by the Tahsildar. Farming is not permitted on government or forest department land.</string>
    <string name="legal_q3_title">Register Your Plantation</string>
    <string name="legal_q3_content">After planting you must register each sandalwood tree with your local District Forest Officer or Divisional Forest Officer. Carry your land ownership documents and a list of trees with their GPS locations. Registration fee varies by district. Keep the registration receipt safely — it is your proof of legal ownership.</string>
    <string name="legal_q4_title">Subsidies and Loans</string>
    <string name="legal_q4_content">Karnataka offers free or subsidized sandalwood saplings through the Sirichandana Vana scheme and Krishi Aranya Yojana. Contact your local horticulture department office for eligibility. Agricultural banks offer low-interest loans for sandalwood plantation under Kisan Credit schemes.</string>
    
